---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import GoalIndex from '../../../components/GoalIndex.astro';
import ProcessFlow from '../../../components/ProcessFlow.astro';
import ToolCard from '../../../components/ToolCard.astro';
import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';
import { tools } from '../../../data/tools';
import { goalsByComponent } from '../../../data/goals';
import { componentById } from '../../../data/components';
import { url } from '../../../utils/url';

const comp = componentById['language'];

const problems = [
  {
    title: 'Meaning gets lost in translation',
    body: 'A phrase can shift with the language, the tone, the region, or the gesture that goes with it. Word-for-word translation keeps the words and drops the rest: rapport, tone, code-switching, and the gap between what someone says and what they mean.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Every finding looks equally certain',
    body: 'Nothing in a typical research pipeline marks whether a finding is what a person meant or one interpreter’s best guess. It travels with the same authority however many hops of translation or single-source judgment calls produced it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Generic personas result',
    body: 'By the time a finding reaches a design team, meaning has quietly become approximation, and the interface gets built for an averaged, invented person rather than anyone real.',
  },
];

const applies = [
  'Public-service and civic platforms in any linguistically diverse country',
  'Healthcare intake and patient-facing tools used with immigrant or refugee populations',
  'Financial-inclusion products, banking apps, and lending platforms',
  'E-commerce, delivery, and logistics across dialect-rich regions',
  'Humanitarian and NGO field tools in crisis zones with no shared language',
  'Enterprise software used by distributed teams working in a second or third language',
  'Voice assistants, IVR systems, and chatbots deployed far outside the dialect they were trained on',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={comp.name}
  description="Practical tools for capturing emotional and cultural meaning faithfully in cross-language field research, tagging how reliable each finding is, and carrying it through to interface wording that has actually been tested."
  component="c3"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Components', href: '/components/' }, { label: '3 · Language' }]} />
      <div class="chip-row chips">
        <span class="chip chip--quiet">Component 3</span>
        <span class="chip chip--quiet">Meaning-to-Interface Toolkit</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Keep what people mean, not only what the words say.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Practical tools for capturing emotional and cultural meaning faithfully in cross-language field research, tagging how
        reliable each finding is, and carrying it through to interface wording that has actually been tested.
      </p>
      <p class="built">
        Built for research on first-generation Indian college students. Reusable for any cross-language qualitative research.
      </p>
      <p class="btns">
        <a class="btn btn--accent" href="#c3-goals">Find your tool <Icon name="arrow" size={18} /></a>
        <a class="btn btn--outline" href={url('/components/language/methodology/')}>Read the methodology</a>
        <a class="btn btn--outline" href={url('/downloads/#language')}><Icon name="download" size={18} /> Templates</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section section--white" aria-labelledby="c3-goals-h">
    <div class="wrap">
      <GoalIndex
        groups={goalsByComponent('c3')}
        lead="Start with what you’re doing. Every tool works on its own."
        search={false}
        showComponent={false}
        id="c3-goals"
        headingId="c3-goals-h"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="problem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eb">The problem</p>
        <h2 id="problem">Cross-language research loses exactly the thing that matters most.</h2>
        <p>
          Run a conversation through a translator or transcriber once and the nuance is gone by the time it reaches a design team.
          Nobody downstream can tell what was meant from what was guessed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        {
          problems.map((p) => (
            <article class="card">
              <h3>{p.title}</h3>
              <p>{p.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--white" id="process" aria-labelledby="process-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eb">The process</p>
        <h2 id="process-heading">Three modules, an entry step, and a loop.</h2>
        <p>
          Module 1 builds a picture of the setting. Module 2 captures meaning moments one person at a time. Module 3 turns raw
          moments into validated meaning, then into a tested design response. The last step feeds new questions back into
          Module 1, so the process compounds across research cycles.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ProcessFlow />
      <p class="funnel">
        Not every Meaning Card travels all the way to a shipped pattern. Many surface something useful without further action, a
        smaller number justify a design hypothesis, and fewer still become tested, library-worthy patterns. A “not yet” or a
        “no” at a decision point is the toolkit doing its job.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="all-tools">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eb">Tools</p>
        <h2 id="all-tools">The six tools, in the order you meet them</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--3">
        {tools.map((t, i) => <ToolCard tool={t} index={i + 1} />)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--white" aria-labelledby="where">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <div>
        <p class="eb">Where to use it</p>
        <h2 id="where">Not specific to one sector, or to India.</h2>
        <p>
          The condition that makes this component necessary is simple: somewhere between the people building a product and the
          people using it, there is a language or meaning gap, and someone is bridging it by guesswork rather than a documented
          method.
        </p>
        <p>
          The common thread is a research or design team on one side of a language boundary and real users on the other, with no
          method in place for checking whether meaning crossed.
        </p>
        <p><a class="link-arrow" href={url('/components/language/methodology/')}>Read the methodology <Icon name="arrow" size={18} /></a></p>
      </div>
      <ul class="applies">
        {applies.map((a) => <li>{a}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .chips {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .page-head .lead {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .built {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .btns {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .funnel {
    max-width: var(--prose);
    margin: 2rem 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem 3.5rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
    align-items: start;
  }
  .applies {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  .applies li {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    background: var(--ground);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: 0.97rem;
  }
</style>
